data.fused_content:
Southern Asia has been hit by a series of catastrophic floods and landslides that have claimed more than 900 lives across the region. In Sri Lanka, Cyclone Ditwah has produced severe flooding in low‑lying areas of Colombo and has caused the death toll to rise to 334, with many people still missing. The disaster is described as the island’s worst natural event in the past two decades, following the 2004 tsunami that killed around 31,000 people. President Anura Dissanayake declared a state of emergency and pledged to rebuild the country with international assistance, stating that the nation would emerge stronger.

In Indonesia, authorities report that more than 442 people have died and 402 remain missing on Sumatra island. The hardest‑hit districts include Sibolga and Central Tapanuli in North Sumatra. Heavy rains triggered landslides, damaged roads and cut communication lines, leaving thousands stranded without food, water or medical supplies. Two warships have been dispatched from Jakarta to deliver aid to areas that remain inaccessible. Police forces were mobilised after incidents of shop break‑ins were reported, and the police spokesperson Ferry Walintukan said that looting occurred before logistical aid arrived.

In both countries, residents have resorted to building tents beside the remaining walls of their homes. Videos circulating on social media show people wading through waist‑deep floodwater to reach damaged convenience stores, scrambling past crumbling barricades and broken glass to obtain food, medicine and fuel. Local officials have not yet begun clearing roads, and external assistance has not yet reached many of the most affected communities.

Thailand is also working urgently to remove debris and locate missing persons after its own floods and landslides, although specific casualty figures have not yet been released. The combined crises underscore how the climate crisis is altering storm patterns and increasing the frequency of extreme weather events across southern Asia.
