Deputy Minister Phùng Đức Tiên said that Vietnam may still face a 16th storm as 2025 draws to a close.  
Deputy Director Hoàng Đức Cường of the Hydrometeorological Department gave an overview of the year’s natural‑disaster and hydro‑meteorological conditions, noting that 2025 has seen exceptionally atypical weather and disaster patterns that caused significant damage across the country.

A record 1,739 mm of rainfall fell at the Bach Ma station in Thừa Thiên‑Huế in a single day in October, a figure the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is evaluating as one of the greatest 24‑hour totals ever recorded worldwide.

Storm activity in the East Sea has reached its highest level since Vietnam began systematic record‑keeping in 1961. By the end of November, 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented, surpassing the previous record of 20 set in 2017. In June, Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) emerged in the East Sea—a phenomenon not witnessed in more than four decades. Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) achieved level 17 wind speeds, with gusts exceeding this level, making it the most powerful storm ever recorded in the East Sea. A tropical depression that moved from the Indian Ocean to the north‑western Pacific in late November followed an unprecedented path.

Flooding in 2025 was extensive and intense. River levels reached unprecedented heights across numerous areas, with 20 rivers recording either extreme or historic flooding. The Cau River (Thai Nguyen, Bắc Ninh), Thuong River (Bắc Giang), Bang Giang River (Cao Bang), and Lo River (Tuyên Quang) were among those that overflowed. Floods stretched from the northern and central territories to the central highlands, further impacting the lower Mekong Delta. A historic flood in eastern Đắk Lắk—once part of Phú Yên province—surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin.

The pattern of intensifying weather extremes, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding, is becoming more evident and is directly linked to climate change. These developments indicate a shift in conventional flood patterns in Vietnam.
