On Saturday, rescue teams in Indonesia struggled to reach victims in several provinces that were devastated by an earthquake and the ensuing floods. The confirmed death toll rose to 248, with more than 500 people injured, officials said, and they warned that the figure could climb further as many bodies remain unaccounted for.

In West Sumatra, the death toll reached 74 by Saturday, and rescuers had recovered additional bodies in Agam district. However, 78 people are still missing in three villages of Agam, and the area remains difficult to access because of mud, rocks and the lack of heavy machinery. Relatives watched as rescuers pulled mud‑caked bodies from a buried house in Salareh Aia village.

The disaster has isolated many communities. Roads and bridges were washed out or collapsed—nine bridges were reported to have fallen, cutting off traffic between Medan in North Sumatra and Banda Aceh. Communication lines were disrupted, forcing relief aircraft to deliver essential aid and supplies to the severely affected Central Tapanuli district and other parts of the region.

Heavy monsoon rains over the past week caused rivers to overflow in North Sumatra, flooding thousands of homes and ravaging a mountainside village. Flash floods hit Bireuen district in Aceh, and officials in Aceh province said rescuers were struggling to reach victims. The governor of Aceh, Muzakir Manaf, declared a state of emergency until December 11 to address the crisis.

Hundreds of police, soldiers and local residents sifted through wreckage with bare hands, shovels and hoes, while the rain hampered their progress. “The death toll is presumed to be on the rise, as numerous bodies remain unaccounted for and several are still missing,” said Suharyanto, chief of the National Disaster Management Agency (BNPB).

Close to 3,000 displaced families have sought refuge in government shelters. The affected regions, situated along the “Ring of Fire,” are frequently impacted by earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and tsunamis, adding to the urgency of the relief effort.
