DMC Director‑General Sampath Kotuwegoda said the military – army, navy and air force – had been deployed to strengthen relief operations. As the cyclone tracked north toward India, the government urged international assistance and called on Sri Lankans abroad to donate for nearly half a million affected people.

The cyclone’s northward path triggered new landslides in Kandy, 115 km east of Colombo, flooding sections of the main access road, and worsening floods in low‑lying areas forced evacuations along the Kelani River.

India responded swiftly, sending two aircraft loaded with relief items and a warship that docked in Colombo for a goodwill visit, providing provisions and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment. Prime Minister Narendra Modi expressed his condolences on X, lamenting the loss of life and reaffirming India’s commitment to aid its “closest maritime neighbour.”
