Vietnam’s central coastal region has been struck by unprecedented rainfall, with the environment ministry reporting that more than one metre of rain fell in a single day, submerging the historic city of Hue, a UNESCO World Heritage site. Since Saturday, over 8,600 residents across four central provinces have taken refuge in schools and other public facilities, driven by threats of severe flooding and landslides.

The deluge forced the closure of educational institutions in Hue and Danang, and the flooding disrupted the railway line that connects the northern and southern parts of the country. In the nearby town of Hoi An, state media footage captured tourists maneuvering boats through the town’s narrow lanes, while AFP journalists reported on evacuation efforts in the most inundated areas.

Experts attribute the increasing lethality and destruction of extreme weather events, such as storms and floods, to climate change driven by human activities. Earlier in October, Vietnam’s rugged northern regions and its capital, Hanoi, experienced severe flooding caused by typhoons Bualoi and Matmo.
