The national disaster agency reported that the death toll from floods and landslides on Sumatra island has risen to 708, with 504 people still missing. The figure had been initially listed as 753 but was later adjusted to 708 by Tuesday evening, following an earlier count of 604 fatalities on Monday.

The devastation has been driven by heavy monsoon rains and a rare tropical storm that formed in the Malacca Strait. The storm caused widespread flooding and landslides across Sumatra and southern Thailand, where 181 people were killed. In total, more than 1,300 lives have been lost in the region, and the floods have damaged infrastructure and inundated towns.

Approximately 3.2 million people in Indonesia have been affected, 2,600 have suffered injuries, and about one million residents have been evacuated from high‑risk zones. Communities in Aceh face severe food shortages and hunger if supply lines are not restored within the next week.

Aid efforts are hampered by impassable roads and destroyed bridges. UNICEF reports that areas such as Tapanuli Tengah in North Sumatra and Agam in Aceh are inaccessible by road. Islamic Relief is dispatching 12 tonnes of emergency food supplies, while the Indonesian government has announced the delivery of 34,000 tonnes of rice and 6.8 million litres of cooking oil to Aceh, North Sumatra and West Sumatra. The charity warned that if supply chains are not re‑established within a week, communities could face severe hunger.

The World Health Organization is sending rapid response teams and essential supplies to the affected area and is enhancing disease monitoring. WHO Director‑General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said in Geneva that the situation underscores how climate change is leading to more frequent and intense weather events with devastating consequences.

Survivors in evacuation centres have recounted the sudden onslaught of water. “We didn’t think we would survive that night because the situation was so chaotic. Everyone was thinking about saving themselves. There was no prior warning whatsoever before the water came,” said Gahitsa Zahira Cahyani, a 17‑year‑old student at an Islamic boarding school. Hundreds of students fled during the night, some clinging to trees and the mosque’s roof.

Although rainfall has eased nationwide, officials say that landslide alerts remain in force across most of the hardest‑hit central region.
