Singapore: Over 1,200 lives have been claimed by severe weather in southern Asia over the past two weeks, and the toll is expected to rise as rescuers search for hundreds still missing.

Late last month, simultaneous tropical storms triggered floods and mudslides that obliterated entire villages in Sri Lanka and on the Indonesian island of Sumatra. In Sri Lanka, Cyclone Ditwah lingered along the eastern coastline for more than two days starting late last week, killing at least 390 people and leaving hundreds unaccounted for. The disaster has forced about 180,000 people to seek refuge in government‑designated safety centres, and the National Disaster Management Agency reports that roughly 1 million residents across 25 districts have been affected.

In Indonesia, Cyclone Senyar emerged over the Malacca Strait in the middle of last week and unleashed a month’s worth of rain—382 millimetres—in a single 24‑hour period beginning on 25 November. The storm interacted with Typhoon Koto over the Philippines, intensified the rainfall, and caused widespread flooding. The National Disaster Management Agency says nearly 1.5 million people in 48 regencies of Sumatra have been impacted, with more than 500,000 displaced. The death toll in Aceh, North Sumatra and West Sumatra has risen to 659.

Hat Yai, a city in southern Thailand, experienced a once‑in‑300‑year rainfall event, with over 600 millimetres falling across three days starting 19 November. The city and surrounding provinces have reported at least 181 fatalities, mainly in Songkhla, and locals have expressed disapproval of the new national administration for failing to provide adequate warnings.

Indonesian President Prabowo Subianto has urged stronger measures to combat climate change, saying “We must confront climate change effectively” as he toured the affected regions. Sri Lankan President Anura Kumara Dissanayake described the current crisis as “the largest and most challenging natural disaster” the nation has ever faced, noting that it is unprecedented for such a calamity to impact the whole country.

Rescue teams continue to comb through mud and wreckage in search of survivors, and officials warn that additional severe weather events are expected as climate change progresses.
