The Australian Labor government has announced that it will allocate $200 million to 96 projects across the country to strengthen community resilience against severe weather.  The funding is part of the third round of the $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), which now totals $600 million in this round, bringing the cumulative federal investment to roughly $400 million for more than 350 projects in earlier rounds.

The new projects cover a range of states and territories.  On Palm Island off Queensland’s coast, a cyclone shelter and evacuation centre that can accommodate 800 people will be built.  In Central New South Wales, a $14.1 million investment will upgrade stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera.  South Australia will receive $1.6 million for an infrastructure project at Townsvale Estate, while northwest Western Australia will get $895 k to enhance the capacity of the Karajarri Rangers, a community group focused on wildfire prevention.

Other initiatives include the establishment of recovery centres, flood‑protection infrastructure and advanced alert systems for natural threats.  According to the department, almost half of the projects funded by the DRF are in pre‑construction or construction delivery, and 43 projects are already approaching completion or have been finished.

The DRF, launched by the Albanese government to mitigate the impacts of floods, bushfires, cyclones and other extreme weather events, aims to make Australia more resilient.  The Labor government says the third round will benefit all 538 local government areas across the nation.
