Unpredictable weather patterns in Korea have triggered an unusual phenomenon that has devastated apple harvests. Farmers in South Jeolla Province report that many apples have split open—a process they call “yeolgwa.” The sudden absorption of water overwhelms the fruit’s skin, causing it to crack.

Yoon Young‑hwan, who has grown apples since 1972, said he had 100 apples last year, about 80 of which split. This year, despite his efforts, roughly 60 % of his apples cracked. “We call this ‘yeolgwa,’” he told The Korea Times, noting that the concept did not exist when he first started farming.

Apples are a cool‑temperature crop that thrives when air temperatures range between 45 and 52 °F, the publication reported. Climate change, driven by continued reliance on non‑renewable energy and the release of harmful gases, is intensifying extreme weather events that threaten agricultural stability.

The industry faces the risk of collapse. Switching to subtropical fruits or importing apples is both costly and time‑consuming, and Yoon expressed frustration that the country imports apples while local growers cannot produce them reliably.
