Typhoon Kalmaegi, with sustained winds of about 114 mph and gusts up to 137 mph, swept across the South China Sea in mid‑November, delivering more than 24 inches of rain in some areas.  Meteorologists said the storm dumped the equivalent of one‑and‑a‑half months of rainfall in a single day over metropolitan Cebu, triggering flash floods, swollen rivers and the loss of numerous vehicles.

In Vietnam, the central provinces are battling severe flooding after record‑breaking rainfall.  Coastal cities such as Danang saw waves up to 10 feet high, while Quy Nhon experienced prolonged power outages.  High tides on the Saigon River and strong winds toppled trees in Dak Lak, and officials warned that up to 4 inches of rain could flood low‑lying areas.

The Philippines has suffered the most devastating impact.  The typhoon killed at least 114 people and left 127 missing, with 71 fatalities reported in Cebu alone.  An additional 62 people were declared missing in Negros Occidental, close to Cebu.  The Office of Civil Defence said the storm displaced more than 560,000 villagers, about 450,000 of whom were moved to emergency shelters.  A Philippine Air Force helicopter crashed in Agusan del Sur on Tuesday, killing six crew members who were en route to deliver aid.  Cebu is still recovering from a 6.9‑magnitude earthquake that struck on September 30, which caused at least 79 deaths and displaced many residents.

President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. declared a state of emergency on Thursday, allowing the government to expedite emergency funds and curb food hoarding and price gouging.  Officials warned that a new tropical cyclone emerging from the Pacific could intensify into a super typhoon and threaten the northern Philippines early next week.

Governor Pamela Baricuatro said the storm’s impact may have been worsened by quarrying activities that blocked rivers and by inadequate flood‑control measures in Cebu province.  The scandal over missing or corrupt flood‑control projects has sparked public anger and street demonstrations in recent months.

The typhoon exited the Philippine archipelago on Wednesday, heading into the South China Sea, but its aftereffects—mass displacement, widespread flooding, and ongoing recovery from the recent earthquake—continue to strain local authorities and communities.
