The World Meteorological Organization (WMO), a United Nations agency, urged governments to strengthen early‑warning systems for extreme weather at its annual conference in Geneva. “Early warning means early action,” Secretary‑General Celeste Saulo said, stressing that timely alerts can save lives.

The WMO noted that almost 50 % of countries are highly exposed to severe weather events such as hurricanes, floods, wildfires and glacier collapses, and that developing nations are especially vulnerable. Over the past five decades, climate‑related incidents have caused more than 2 million deaths worldwide, the agency added. It warned that many countries still lack adequate safeguards, and that implementing early‑warning systems could avert most of the fatalities caused by such conditions.

An early‑warning system provides additional time for evacuation and preparation, allowing people to reinforce or abandon their homes before a disaster strikes. Yet only half of the countries that use these systems employ them beyond basic features, and 16 % operate below even those minimum levels.

The WMO highlighted ongoing progress in establishing operational systems, but emphasized that much remains to be done. It called for broader implementation of early‑warning networks, noting that effective systems in more nations would preserve more lives. The agency is focused on raising awareness of the underuse of early‑warning technology for extreme weather and on encouraging further efforts to expand coverage worldwide.
