COLOMBO, Dec. 9 (Xinhua) – Humanitarian flood relief supplies sent by the Chinese government reached Colombo on Monday morning and were officially handed over to Sri Lankan authorities. The ceremony was attended by Sri Lanka’s Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunathilake, Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Qi Zhenhong, and Deputy Minister of Defence Aruna Jayasekera.  

Karunathilake thanked China for its prompt and generous assistance, noting that China has consistently supported Sri Lanka during difficult times and that Chinese businesses and the Chinese community in Sri Lanka have also contributed to relief efforts. Qi Zhenhong said that all relevant Chinese agencies acted swiftly to offer help and that Chinese enterprises and the diaspora have been actively involved in local disaster relief.  

The aid package, which includes tents, bed sheets, life jackets and blankets, was described as meeting the country’s critical needs. Sri Lanka’s Disaster Management Center said the cyclone, named Ditwah, has demolished over 4,000 homes and partially damaged more than 60,000 residences across the country. China remains committed to extending support to Sri Lanka to the best of its ability, as it has done in the past.
