Colombo – Sri Lanka has declared a state of emergency after Cyclone Ditwah triggered torrential rains and widespread flooding that have killed 132 people and left 176 missing.  

The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reports that more than 15,000 homes have been destroyed and 78,000 residents have been moved to government‑managed temporary shelters.  

President Anura Kumara Dissanayake has enacted emergency legislation, giving him broad powers to coordinate relief efforts.  The DMC’s Director‑General, Sampath Kotuwegoda, said the army, navy and air force are now fully engaged in rescue and support operations.  

On Saturday the military evacuated 69 bus passengers, including a German tourist, from the Anuradhapura district.  A helicopter had to abort its first rescue attempt because the rotor’s downdraft risked dislodging the roof on which the passengers were perched; naval boats subsequently reached the stranded group.  

In the central district of Badulla, landslides have blocked roads, isolating villages such as Maspanna.  Saman Kumara of Maspanna reported that the village has no food, limited clean water and that the collapse of power lines and flooding of water‑purification facilities have left about one‑third of the country without electricity or running water.  

Cyclone Ditwah departed the island on Saturday, heading north toward India.  Chennai Airport has grounded 54 flights in anticipation of heavy rain and strong winds.  New landslides in Kandy’s central district have submerged sections of the main access road.  

The government has appealed for international assistance and urged Sri Lankans living abroad to contribute financially.  Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya met with diplomats in Colombo to brief them on the situation and request support.  India was the first to respond, sending aircraft loaded with relief supplies and docking a warship in Colombo that offered provisions to the affected.  Prime Minister Narendra Modi expressed condolences for the loss of life and confirmed that New Delhi is prepared to provide additional aid.  

Authorities have issued evacuation orders for residents along the banks of the Kelani River, a low‑lying area that has been heavily inundated.  While rainfall has eased in most of the country, the northern region continues to receive showers as a lingering effect of the cyclone.  

Since 2017, when floods and landslides claimed over 200 lives and forced hundreds of thousands to seek shelter, Cyclone Ditwah has become the most lethal natural disaster to strike Sri Lanka.  The 2003 floods had previously been the country’s worst, killing 254 people.
