Extreme weather conditions in the Greater Victoria area have triggered an Emergency Weather Response, with shelters opened in both Victoria and Saanich to protect residents from freezing temperatures and strong winds.

In Saanich, the shelter at 3703 St. Aidan’s St. is operated by Our Place Society with financial support from the District of Saanich and BC Housing. It offers 25 provisional winter‑shelter spots and 25 fixed sleeping accommodations, accommodates people of all genders, and has a strict no‑pet policy. The shelter will remain open as long as severe weather conditions meet provincial activation standards, and it will operate from 8 p.m. on Friday to 7 a.m. on Saturday.

Residents can also use daytime public warming facilities at the Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. The district notes that operating hours at these venues may change during severe weather due to staffing availability.

In Victoria, the municipality has added 30 additional shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative, providing extra overnight accommodation for those in need.

Forecasts predict winds of up to 30 km/h, with gusts reaching 70 km/h near the Juan de Fuca Strait, and overnight temperatures expected to fall to about 1 °C, with even colder conditions anticipated further inland.
