A severe storm in São Paulo knocked out power for about 1.3 million residents and forced the cancellation of nearly 400 flights.  

According to the Associated Press, the storm produced winds of up to 100 km h⁻¹, causing trees and power lines to fall. Utility Enel initially restored electricity to 1.2 million of its 2.2 million customers, but 300 000 people remained without power on Thursday, leaving roughly 1 million still without electricity.  

São Paulo mayor Ricardo Nunes called the company “irresponsible” and criticized the delay in restoring service. “Every time something happens, we are left behind without electricity,” he said. Psychologist Thais Fernandes told the AP that this was the third outage this year, adding that neither the company nor local authorities could be trusted.  

Bloomberg reported that the state’s commerce federation estimated that businesses and service providers lost about 1.54 billion reais (≈ $290 million) in revenue because of the outages. Similar disputes over Enel’s handling of power cuts surfaced in 2023 and 2024.  

The incidents underscore the vulnerability of São Paulo’s energy grid to extreme weather, which is intensified by rising temperatures. The economic toll is significant, and utility providers are struggling to keep pace with the increasing challenges posed by severe weather.  

Measures to improve resilience include advanced weather monitoring and encouraging customers to adopt solar power with battery storage, which can reduce dependence on the grid and on less sustainable energy sources such as oil and gas.  

The storm did not result in any fatalities, although extreme weather events elsewhere have caused loss of life.
