Victorian government officials are expanding support for families and primary producers affected by the January bushfires, building on earlier disaster‑recovery initiatives. Premier Jacinta Allan announced that uninsured homeowners who have lost their main residence can receive grants of up to $52,250. “The devastation wrought by these fires has been beyond measure for countless individuals across Victoria – which is why we are providing additional financial and psychological aid to alleviate some of the challenges faced by our farmers and regional communities,” she said.

The state is also boosting mental‑health and business assistance for farmers. The “Look Over the Farm Gate” initiative, managed by the National Centre for Farmer Health, will begin on 16 January and offers grants of up to $5,000 for local events and activities that support mental health and community recovery. The Rural Financial Counselling Service will provide free, confidential wellbeing assistance and financial and business counselling. Farmers who cannot safely return to their land can submit requests to Agriculture Victoria, which will coordinate with the Fire Incident Controller to determine when and how they may inspect their animals.

To channel public donations, the Victorian government has launched the 2026 Victorian Bushfire Appeal. The appeal will allocate funds to communities impacted by the fires, with the state covering administrative expenses. Vicki Ward, Minister for Natural Disaster Recovery, said the appeal aims to distribute money based on priorities set by local communities in partnership with charities and community organisations.

Insurance claims are rising. The Insurance Council of Australia reports that insurers have received 1,386 claims since 7 January across home, commercial, motor and business‑interruption policies, with about 30 % of property claims accounting for total losses. Suncorp has activated its extreme‑weather protocol, recording roughly 80 home and motor claims linked to Ex‑Tropical Cyclone Koji and about 60 claims related to the Victorian bushfires. IAG has deployed a mobile claims unit in Seymour and its Major Event Response Teams are visiting affected properties to conduct assessments and urgent repairs, using satellite imagery where ground access is limited.

Health coverage has also been adjusted for those in affected areas. The Health Care Fund (HCF) offers a relief initiative that provides eligible members with a premium waiver of up to three months and the option to pause coverage for up to two years. The initiative also includes the HealthyMinds Check‑in programme, which offers private consultations with certified psychologists, and, through a partnership with GP2U, complimentary 15‑minute online GP consultations for members in rural and remote locations.

Federal Emergency Management Minister Kristy McBain confirmed that the Albanese government is working closely with Victoria throughout both the firefighting and recovery phases, assuring Victorians that national support will continue as the state rebuilds.
