Fatalities from floods and landslides on Sumatra’s western islands have risen to 174, with about 80 people still missing, the National Disaster Management Agency (NDMA) said on Friday.  The toll includes 116 deaths in North Sumatra, 25 in Aceh and 23 in West Sumatra.  More than 3,200 homes and other structures were damaged overall, while West Sumatra alone saw over 17,000 homes flooded and roughly 23,000 residents displaced into temporary shelters.

The disaster was triggered by torrential monsoon rains that began on Tuesday, causing rivers in North Sumatra to overflow and sending mud and rocks into hilly settlements.  The heavy rainfall was intensified by tropical cyclone Senyar, which formed in the Strait of Malacca and produced strong winds and high waves across Aceh, North Sumatra, West Sumatra, Riau and surrounding areas before fading.

Floods and landslides destroyed rice fields, livestock and public facilities, and severed bridges and roads, isolating many communities.  The steep, saturated terrain made the region especially vulnerable to such events.

Achadi Subarkah Raharjo of Indonesia’s Meteorology, Climatology and Geophysical Agency said the extreme‑weather warning has been extended because of the abundant water vapor supply and shifting atmospheric dynamics.  He cautioned that unstable atmospheric conditions could prolong the extreme weather as long as the cyclone system remains active.
