COLOMBO – Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday after Cyclone Ditwah caused widespread devastation. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that more than 20,000 homes were destroyed, 108,000 people were housed in government‑run temporary shelters, and 798,000 individuals were temporarily displaced. The death toll has reached 153, with 191 people missing.

Rescue and relief operations have involved the army, navy, air force, civilian workers and volunteers. A passenger, now hospitalized, recounted how navy sailors guided him onto a nearby roof using ropes to navigate the rising floodwaters. A helicopter’s initial rescue attempt was aborted because its rotor downdraft risked dislodging the roof; naval boats subsequently completed the rescue. Internet connections have been disrupted, and clean water is scarce.

In the central district of Badulla, roads remain impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. About a third of the country is without electricity or running water, as collapsed power lines and inundated water‑purification facilities have cut off services. Saman Kumara from Maspanna in Badulla reported that villagers cannot leave or be reached, that food supplies are dwindling, and that clean water is running out.

Cyclone Ditwah moved away from the island on Saturday, heading toward neighbouring India. In response, Chennai Airport cancelled 54 flights, and meteorologists forecast extremely heavy rainfall and strong winds for the next 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan government has appealed for international aid and urged Sri Lankans abroad to make cash donations to support affected communities. An Indian warship, already in Colombo on a pre‑scheduled goodwill visit, donated its rations to assist the victims.
