World Weather Attribution (WWA) released its annual report, *Unequal Evidence and Impacts, Limits to Adaptation: Extreme Weather in 2025*, on 30 December 2025. The study found that global temperatures remained unusually high throughout 2025, making it one of the three hottest years on record and the first year the 1.5 °C threshold was crossed since the Paris Agreement in 2015.

The report documented 157 extreme weather events that met its humanitarian‑impact criteria, including 49 floods, 49 heatwaves, 38 storms, 11 wildfires, 7 droughts and 3 cold spells. Heatwaves were the deadliest, with an estimated 24 400 deaths in Europe alone, while storms and tropical cyclones killed more than 1 700 people worldwide. Heatwaves have intensified up to tenfold compared to a decade earlier, the report noted.

Attribution studies linked the increased frequency and severity of these events to human‑driven climate change. Heatwaves in South Sudan, Burkina Faso, Norway, Sweden, Mexico, Argentina and England were intensified, and all Atlantic hurricanes in 2025 were found to have been stronger because of warmer oceans. Wildfires in Los Angeles and southern Spain were also more likely under current climate conditions.

The report highlighted that the most vulnerable—poor and marginalised communities—suffered the greatest losses. Regions such as central Africa, western Australia, central Brazil, Canada and large parts of the Middle East experienced some of their driest years on record, leading to water scarcity, agricultural setbacks and heightened wildfire risk.

Experts warned that adaptation alone is insufficient. “The ongoing escalation of greenhouse gas emissions has driven our climate into a heightened state of extremity, where even minor upticks in global temperatures lead to disproportionately severe consequences,” said a WWA spokesperson. “Unless fossil fuels are swiftly phased out, extreme weather events will grow more severe, challenging societies beyond their capacity to manage.”

Friederike Otto, climate‑science professor at Imperial College London, noted that the threats posed by climate change have shifted from theoretical to starkly tangible. Sjoukje Philip of the Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute added that the extent of warming observed in 2025 cannot be attributed solely to natural climate variability.

The report calls for urgent action to reduce fossil‑fuel consumption and strengthen resilience, emphasizing that without swift decarbonisation, the world will face increasingly catastrophic weather events.
