The most expensive disaster of the year was the January wildfires that swept through Los Angeles, which inflicted roughly $61.2 billion in damage—nearly twice the amount of the previous record holder.  In March, a massive tornado outbreak that stretched from Texas and Oklahoma across the Gulf Coast and into North Carolina produced 180 tornadoes, claimed 43 lives, and caused $11 billion in damage, making it the largest March outbreak on record.

A severe drought in the western United States, fueled by intense heat, added another $3.1 billion in economic damage.  Overall, severe weather events accounted for 21 of the 23 billion‑dollar disasters, roughly 91 % of the total losses.

The interval between catastrophic events has shortened dramatically.  While the average gap in the 1980s was about 82 days, the 2025 average was only 10 days, underscoring the increasing frequency of extreme weather.

Shel Winkley, a meteorologist with Climate Central, said, “Climate change is intensifying extreme weather events and the associated costs continue to rise.”  She added that the impacts affect everyone—from higher insurance premiums to the allocation of public funds for disaster aid and the upheaval of lives and livelihoods.
