JAKARTA – Severe storms that have battered North Sumatra, West Sumatra and Aceh have killed more than 400 people and displaced 290,000 residents, the National Disaster Mitigation Agency (BNPB) said on Saturday.  The agency confirmed 442 deaths and reported that 402 people remain missing.

The flooding and landslides were triggered by Cyclone Senyar and Cyclone Koto, two rare tropical cyclones that struck the region in late November.  Experts say the increasing frequency of such storms is linked to climate change.

BNPB, together with the National Search and Rescue Agency, the military and the police, has led evacuation and relief operations.  The government has supplied electric generators and Starlink communication units to local authorities, shelters, and security forces, and has flown emergency supplies on three Hercules C‑130 aircraft and an A‑400 plane.

Responders face difficult conditions.  Landslides have blocked high‑land roads, while power outages and disrupted communications hamper rescue efforts.  Unstable weather and soil conditions add to the challenge, according to Hery Marantika, head of the Search and Rescue Office in Medan.

Environmental factors have exacerbated the disaster.  The upstream and riverbank areas of Central Tapanuli, the hardest‑hit regency, have lost much land cover due to deforestation for the Batang Toru hydropower project, local logging and gold mining.

The central government has not declared a national disaster, leaving the status to provincial or regency authorities.  Analysts warn that earlier budget cuts to the Meteorology, Climatology and Geophysics Agency (BMKG) and other disaster‑management agencies have weakened the country’s monitoring and early‑warning capacity.
