A 515‑mile lightning flash that stretched from eastern Texas to near Kansas City was identified by NOAA’s GOES‑16 satellite on October 22, 2017, setting a new world record for the longest known lightning strike. The bolt, classified by the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) as a “megaflash,” surpassed the previous record of 477 miles that had been attributed to a 2020 event.

NOAA issued a press release on August 5 confirming the 2017 strike as the longest. The WMO noted that the distance is roughly equivalent to the span between Paris and Venice, and that a car would need eight to nine hours to travel it while a commercial flight would take at least 90 minutes.

Professor Randall Cerveny of the WMO’s Weather and Climate Extremes program said that advances in science and technology now allow precise measurement of such extreme events and that more high‑quality lightning data will become available in the future.

GOES‑16, launched in 2016 as part of the GOES‑R satellite series, has been positioned over the same point in geostationary orbit and has captured images of megaflashes in both 2017 and 2020.

In the broader context of extreme weather, NOAA reports that the United States experiences about 25 million cloud‑to‑ground lightning strikes each year, which are linked to 24 000 fatalities and 240 000 injuries. The National Centers for Environmental Information recorded 27 significant natural disasters in 2024, including 17 severe storms, five tropical cyclones and two winter storms, with total costs of $182.7 billion. The agency’s January report projects that the financial impact of natural disasters could rise by several billion dollars compared with 2024. Wildfires, droughts and heatwaves continue to contribute to the growing number of billion‑dollar events, which reached a record 28 in 2023. NOAA and NASA collaborate to monitor lightning and improve weather forecasting and understanding.
