Pierce County officials in Washington are using a public survey to track the effects of rising sea levels on the region’s coast. The online questionnaire, released by county authorities, invites residents to share their personal experiences and views on how sea‑level rise is impacting local flooding.

The Purdy Sand Spit—known locally simply as “the Spit”—is one of the county’s most vulnerable areas. Residents report that the spit floods at least once a year, often during king tides and storms, and that the flooding has become more severe and unprecedented. “Residents frequently express to us that they are facing unprecedented levels of flooding,” said Councilmember Robyn Denson, according to Gig Harbor Now. The county hopes the hyper‑local data gathered through the survey will help strengthen defenses against future flooding.

Planners warn that the problem is likely to worsen. “By 2050, the region might experience severe flooding annually,” said planner Ansley Silva. Rising sea levels are driven by global warming, which expands ocean water and increases the volume of water that can inundate coastal zones like the Spit.

To address the threat, Pierce County is encouraging all residents to complete the survey. In other regions, experts are exploring wetland rehabilitation as a natural barrier against floodwaters, and many communities are mandated to enhance their coastal infrastructure to improve resilience. The county also urges residents to support planet‑protecting policies that reduce pollution from oil, gas, and other energy sources.

Residents who live in Pierce County are invited to contribute their insights by participating in the sea‑level rise survey, helping the county build a more informed and prepared response to climate‑related flooding.
