The dzud, a severe climatic phenomenon in Mongolia, occurs when a summer drought is followed by an exceptionally harsh winter. Temperatures can plunge below –30 °C, and heavy snowfall can cover the landscape, turning the country into a white wasteland. Climate‑change trends have altered the frequency of dzuds, with some regions now experiencing them annually, and the disaster has become both a natural calamity and a socio‑economic crisis.

The winter of 2022‑2023 was the harshest on record. About 90 % of Mongolia’s land was shrouded in thick snow, the heaviest snowfall in 49 years. Millions of livestock perished from the severe cold and from malnutrition caused by summer droughts that severely limited food production. The loss of animals translates directly into loss of livelihood for herding communities, which rely on livestock for food and income. According to the World Health Organization, 5.9 million people died in 2023 because of the dzud, and 9 % of the country’s entire herd was lost.

When the snow eventually melted, the resulting runoff triggered widespread flooding across the country. The floods damaged infrastructure and grazing areas, and heightened the risk of food‑ and water‑borne illnesses, especially in impoverished regions with rudimentary sanitation systems.

In response, the World Health Organization, the World Bank, and the Mongolian Red Cross Society have coordinated extensive aid. Front‑line health workers have delivered medical supplies and psychological support to residents in the 21 provinces most affected. Weather data is used to trigger the release of disaster‑risk financing before catastrophic events, ensuring herding families receive financial assistance before a dzud begins. The World Bank is working with meteorological and environmental monitoring agencies to compile a comprehensive disaster‑risk financing strategy. One year after the 2024 dzud, the Mongolian Red Cross Society has continued to help herder households recover, providing critical humanitarian assistance and psychological support to those hardest hit.

These efforts demonstrate that early intervention, social‑protection programs, and community support can make a tangible difference in mitigating the impacts of Mongolia’s increasingly frequent dzuds.
