data.fused_content:
**Extreme Weather Drives Mental Health Crisis Among UK Farmers, Study Finds**

A study commissioned by the Energy & Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) shows that extreme weather over the last five years has had a profound impact on the mental health of British farmers. According to the research, 98 % of farmers have experienced severe weather events during this period.

The report highlights a sharp decline in well‑being: 92 % of farmers report anxiety, 34 % feel “very anxious,” 60 % experience depression, and 6 % describe themselves as “very depressed.” Nearly half of respondents (51 %) say that worries about extreme weather occupy their thoughts on a weekly basis, while almost one in five (19 %) report the same concern almost daily.

Economic pressures compound the psychological toll. Forty‑three per cent of farmers fear they may not earn enough to sustain their operations during severe weather. Sixty‑eight per cent are concerned about the costs of recovery, 78 % worry about the loss of crops or livestock, and 52 % are anxious about which crops to plant in an unpredictable climate.

The most frequently encountered weather extremes are extreme rainfall (86 %), drought (78 %), and heatwaves (54 %). Drought has recently been declared in Wales, adding further strain on agricultural producers.

“Erratic weather has made it harder for us to leave the farm, because we might need to deal with a sudden flood or other emergency,” said Joanne Coates, a beef farmer from the Yorkshire Dales. “This discourages people from engaging with community areas, deepening isolation and loneliness.” Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from North Lincolnshire, added that weather “determines the success or failure of a farm,” and the constant uncertainty takes a real toll on mental health.

A spokesperson for the Royal Agricultural Benevolent Institution (RABi) noted that its Big Farming Survey had earlier identified extreme and erratic weather as a principal contributor to stress and deteriorating mental health among farmers. RABi urges those struggling to reach out to its toll‑free helpline at 0800 188 4444.
