The turbulent weather that struck Europe this summer has already inflicted immediate economic damages of at least €43 billion, according to an EU‑wide assessment that has not yet been peer‑reviewed.  The study, released this month, projects that the total cost could rise to €126 billion by 2029 as the full economic toll of the events gradually emerges.

The assessment relies on correlations between meteorological data and economic performance, drawing on an academic paper published earlier this year.  It shows that Cyprus, Greece, Malta and Bulgaria each suffered short‑term declines of more than 1 % in their 2024 gross value added (GVA), a metric comparable to GDP.  Other Mediterranean states—Spain, Italy and Portugal—also experienced significant challenges.

“Extreme weather’s true costs unfold over time, affecting lives and livelihoods through many pathways beyond the immediate aftermath,” said Sehrish Usman, an economist at the University of Mannheim who led the study.  She added that the study’s timely estimates could help policymakers direct aid when official data are unavailable.

Stéphane Hallegatte, chief climate economist at the World Bank and an external commentator, noted that the findings confirm the broader economic repercussions of extreme weather exceed the immediate damage and persist longer than commonly perceived.  He cautioned that the use of “imperfect proxies” for identifying extreme weather events may underestimate total costs, and that GVA figures do not fully capture the impact on individuals and businesses, especially in impoverished communities.

Gert Bijnens, an economist at the National Bank of Belgium, highlighted supply‑chain disruptions as one of the most substantial hidden costs that are often overlooked.  He warned that ignoring these effects could lead to an underestimation of damages by up to 30 %.

The study also points to the intensification of severe weather due to climate change, noting that the likelihood of intense fire conditions has increased 40‑fold in Spain and Portugal and 10‑fold in Greece and Turkey.  The “quietly devastating” heatwave of June is believed to have tripled the fatality rate across 12 major cities, partly driven by pollution that contributes to global warming.

Overall, the research underscores that extreme weather is already creating a significant economic impact in Europe, and that its indirect consequences can be as damaging as the immediate destruction.
