Across Europe, temperatures are rising at twice the global average, a trend that is driving more frequent and severe heatwaves, droughts, floods and storms.  

Recent data from the European Environment Agency show that, despite some advances, environmental conditions are deteriorating.  From 1980 to 2023, climate‑related events have cost Europe more than €738 billion (about £643 billion), with over €162 billion of that loss occurring in the last three years alone.  Europe is the fastest‑warming continent, and the resulting economic and social damages are escalating.  

The human toll is already visible.  More than 464,000 people across Europe have been forcibly displaced by floods, wildfires and storms.  In 2025, severe flooding in Valencia, Spain, and widespread wildfires in Turkey, Portugal, Cyprus, France and Spain highlighted the growing intensity of extreme weather.  

In early October 2023, Storm Amy struck the United Kingdom.  The storm toppled trees and left thousands without electricity for several days, disrupting train services and severing internet connections for many households, particularly in Scotland where the Scottish and Southern Electricity Networks had to restore power to more than 86,000 homes.  

Public services are under mounting strain.  Health facilities, emergency responders and fire brigades are stretched thin, while households, businesses and entire communities face increasing financial and emotional burdens.  

Policy setbacks have compounded the problem.  The European Union has postponed its anti‑deforestation reporting law for a second time, weakening protections for forests that are vital for carbon storage and biodiversity.  

At the same time, several regions are bolstering resilience.  Initiatives prioritise clean energy, a circular economy and the safeguarding of forests, rivers and peatlands.  A €4.2 billion fund supports 77 decarbonisation projects across the EU, and the bloc has already cut greenhouse‑gas emissions by 37 % since 1990.  Europe remains committed to achieving net‑zero emissions by 2050, but success will require coordinated action from governments, businesses and communities alike.
