Heavy rainfall from the southwest monsoon has inundated southern and central Taiwan over the past week, causing widespread flooding, landslides and road closures. The deluge damaged thousands of homes, forced the evacuation of many residents, and led to the shutdown of schools and offices in affected areas.

Meteorological data show that rainfall rates exceeded 100 mm per hour in some locations, with the highest recorded at 122 mm per hour in Sandimen Township, Pingtung County. The Central Weather Administration (CWA) describes rainfall above 80 mm per hour as “creating a sensation of pressure or suffocation, as though the sky is falling; surpassing most urban drainage systems’ capacities; and readily triggering floods and landslides.” A chart posted by the CWA on Facebook links hourly rainfall figures to common perceptions to aid public understanding.

The storm’s winds and heavy rain have caused significant economic damage. Agricultural losses are estimated at more than NT$2.6 billion (US$86.6 million), over 23,000 roofs have been damaged, nearly 2,500 utility poles have fallen, and widespread power outages have occurred.

The typhoon coincided with last month’s intense mass recall campaigns. KMT officials used the event to rally support against the recalls. Taichung Mayor Lu Shiow‑yen of the KMT claimed that DPP lawmakers were focused solely on the campaigns, while Chiayi Mayor Huang Min‑hui of the KMT thanked Kaohsiung Mayor Chen Chi‑mai of the DPP for dispatching personnel to assist Chiayi City’s relief efforts. KMT legislator Niu Hsu‑ting criticized photographs of Taiwan Power Co workers repairing utility poles, calling the images “emotional blackmail” of voters, especially after the KMT had reduced a subsidy for the utility. KMT legislators have also criticized the government’s flood‑management initiatives.

The CWA’s public‑communication efforts, including the Facebook chart, aim to provide clear, science‑based information. Government officials emphasize the need for consistent messaging across channels to restore public confidence and strengthen resilience against future severe weather events.
