data.fused_content:
Turkey’s lemon orchards suffered severe damage this spring after a series of extreme weather events.  Frost first destroyed many blossoms, followed by a fierce hailstorm that struck while the fruits were still immature.  A later heatwave was so intense that the sun’s rays literally boiled the fruit, leading to its demise.  Together, the three calamities caused an estimated 40 percent loss of the crop.

The losses were felt most acutely in the Çukurova valley near Adana, a region that supplies roughly 40 percent of the country’s citrus production.  Mehmet Akın Doğan, president of the Yüreğir Chamber of Agriculture, said farmers in the valley are facing mounting challenges as climate‑change‑driven weather extremes become more frequent.  He added that the situation threatens the stability of food production and security in the area.

Orhan Karaca, a local agricultural leader, remarked that the frost’s impact was “more severe for us than the February 2023 earthquake.”  He noted that farmers are now shifting their focus from salvaging crops to preserving trees, as the damage has become unprecedented in his four decades of apricot cultivation.  The broader agricultural sector, which includes Turkey’s status as the world’s leading exporter of dried apricots, has also been affected by the harsh conditions.

The events underscore a growing trend of extreme weather incidents across Mediterranean countries, driven by accelerating global warming and posing significant threats to rural farming communities.
