A coastal storm that struck the southwestern coast of Alaska on Sunday caused severe flooding in the Native villages of Kipnuk and Kwigillingok, prompting the evacuation of more than 1,500 residents.  One person has been confirmed dead and two others remain missing.

In Kipnuk, ocean water surged 6.6 feet above the usual high tide, breaking the previous record of 4.7 feet set in November 2000.  The surge forced water into the majority of buildings, leaving many homes flooded and some foundations displaced.  The village, located about four miles inland from the coast, has experienced more than 30 flooding events since 1979.

Authorities reported that storm effects were observed in 49 villages across the region.  Cell‑phone service was lost in many of these communities, and the lack of highways linking them to the rest of Alaska compounded the crisis.

The village had received a $20 million grant from the Environmental Protection Agency to construct protective measures, but the funding was withdrawn in May.  Plans to strengthen Kipnuk’s defenses are scheduled to begin next summer, and officials are actively seeking additional financial support.

Local relief groups, including the Western Alaska Disaster Relief Fund and Napakiak Disaster Relief, are collecting donations to assist affected residents.  Residents and officials are urged to contact elected representatives to advocate for continued funding of community safeguards.
