A residence in Camelot Village, Chapel Hill, was severely damaged by flooding caused by Tropical Storm Chantal. The storm claimed six lives in North Carolina and inflicted more than $56 million in damages in Orange County alone, prompting Governor Stein to declare a state of emergency in 13 counties.

The author, a Gulf Coast native, has lived through several major storms. At age three, the family evacuated New Orleans before Hurricane Katrina; the author’s father watched water surge down their street, submerge the front lawn, and creep up the porch, though the water receded before it could enter the house. Later, in 2021, Category 4 Hurricane Ida swept through New Orleans, leaving the city without power for over three weeks at the hottest time of the year. The author also recalls Hurricane Helene, which struck western North Carolina a few weeks after the author moved to Durham for graduate studies, claiming 108 lives and causing damages estimated at $48.8 billion.

The increasing frequency and severity of extreme weather is not random; it is directly attributable to human‑induced climate change. The author notes the president’s claim of investing in technology to protect people from extreme weather events on the same weekend that support for the Federal Emergency Management Agency and renewable energy was slashed, arguing that proactive climate adaptation is essential.

To prevent future tragedies, the author calls for accurate forecasts and warnings, timely federal relief programs, more resilient infrastructure, and comprehensive climate‑action plans. Without concerted environmental efforts, communities will continue to suffer and people will needlessly die. The author urges that voices be heard and that serious steps be taken to address climate change.
