Monsoon rains that began on June 26 have killed at least 225 people and injured more than 500 across Pakistan, rescue officials said.  

Experts say the surge in extreme weather is driven by climate change. A sudden cloudburst on Monday triggered widespread flooding and landslides, burying dozens of vehicles under debris. Heavy equipment is being used to search for missing tourists and residents, and three bodies have been recovered.  

Army helicopters have assisted in evacuating tourists, including women and children, but more than 200 local visitors were stranded when a major highway became blocked.  

The National Disaster Management Authority issued a revised flood warning a few days ago, advising against travel to northern regions because of the risk of landslides and road obstructions. Earlier this month the agency warned that conditions similar to the 2022 floods—which inundated a third of the country and caused 1,737 deaths—cannot be ruled out.
