Specialists at the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) have estimated that the loss of more than 7 million tonnes of wheat this year could have produced over 4 billion loaves of bread – effectively a year’s worth of supply. The shortfall has been driven by a combination of unprecedented heatwaves, severe drought and restrictions on irrigation, all of which have made sowing and crop management extremely difficult for farmers.

The outlook for 2025 points to another challenging harvest. Climate change is expected to bring extended spells of drought followed by intense, heavy rainfall that can cause flooding, further eroding yields. In the past decade the United Kingdom has already experienced two of the three worst harvests on record, with 2020 the worst and 2024 the third‑worst. Wheat self‑sufficiency fell sharply, dropping from 96 % in 2023 to 79 % in 2024, and millers are now importing unprecedented quantities of wheat – twice the five‑year average – to meet demand.

Tom Lancaster, an analyst specialising in land, food and farming at the ECIU, said: “This is the reality of farming amid a shifting climate. Adverse weather conditions are diminishing the British nature of our bread, as millers increasingly rely on imports to compensate for reduced UK production, a trend expected to worsen with the escalation of extreme weather in the years to come.”

Lincolnshire arable farmer Colin Chappell echoed the growing frustration. “I have never known a more challenging time to farm. As a farmer, I am used to managing the worst the weather has to throw at me, but recent years have become impossible,” he said. “I require additional assistance to adapt to these extremes and build the resilience of my business. The closure of green‑farming initiatives in England means that such assistance is unavailable to me. Reopening these programmes must be a priority if farmers are to sustain the cultivation of wheat and other essential crops for the British populace.”

Other crops have also felt the impact. While apple orchards enjoyed an exceptionally fruitful season, this year’s crops endured severe challenges, and winegrowers predict 2025 will be a standout year for vintages, thanks to an abundance of sunny days. The overall picture remains one of financial loss for British farmers, with billions in income evaporating due to increasingly frequent extreme weather conditions.

The need for government support and the reopening of green‑farming initiatives has never been clearer, as the country faces a future where climate‑driven disruptions threaten the stability of its food supply.
