Last month, wildfires that scorched roughly 500,000 hectares (1.2 million acres) across Spain and Portugal were described by scientists as “astonishing” and “30 % stronger than would be expected in a climate‑change‑free world.”  A preliminary analysis by the World Weather Attribution network found that the extreme weather conditions that triggered the fires were made 40 times more probable by climate change.  

The researchers, who based their work on direct weather observations rather than the usual post‑event climate‑model analyses, determined that the combination of heat, dryness and flammability that produced the fires would have occurred only once every 500 years in the pre‑industrial era, but is now expected to happen every 15 years.  They also noted that the 10‑day maximum temperatures recorded in the region last month, previously projected to occur once every 2,500 years, are now anticipated to recur every 13 years.  The study, pending peer review, is part of a broader effort that last week highlighted a ten‑fold increase in the likelihood of similar extreme weather in Turkey and Greece.

Land‑use changes are amplifying the risk.  Rural exodus and an ageing population have left many farms abandoned, allowing dense vegetation to accumulate and become highly flammable.  Applied mathematician David Garcia of the University of Alicante noted that the expansion of vegetation due to reduced rural activity is a key factor in the escalating threat of uncontrolled wildfires.

In response to the growing severity of extreme weather, Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez unveiled a comprehensive 10‑point climate initiative on Monday, aiming to address the challenges posed by climate change.  Sánchez warned that mainstream right‑wing parties might undermine climate action by dismissing scientific evidence, a concern echoed by climate scientists such as Clair Barnes of Imperial College London.
