On Tuesday, a lightning‑ignited wildfire, dubbed the 6‑5 Fire, swept through the historic Gold Rush town of Chinese Camp in Tuolumne County, destroying at least five homes—including standalone and mobile residences—and damaging a recreational vehicle. The blaze, part of the TCU September Lightning Complex that has scorched more than 19 square miles across Calaveras and Tuolumne counties, expanded to 10 square miles before firefighters arrived. Residents cleared branches and tossed sand in an attempt to hold the fire, but the flames spread, prompting the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby roads. CalFire has requested additional resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and aircraft, and the fire remains uncontained as of early Wednesday. No casualties or fatalities have been reported. The 6‑5 Fire is one of more than a dozen fires that ignited across California on Tuesday. Chinese Camp, established around 1850 by Chinese miners expelled from a neighboring camp and originally named Camp Washington, was renamed to honor the influx of Chinese settlers. During the Gold Rush, Chinese miners faced persecution, including an excessive Foreign Miners Tax designed to drive them out of mining.
