A powerful earthquake struck the Kamchatka region on Sunday, following an 8.8‑magnitude quake that had hit the coast earlier that week. The event occurred at a depth of about 12 miles (19 km) and its epicenter lay roughly 74 miles east‑southeast of Petropavlovsk‑Kamchatsky. The U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) initially reported a magnitude of 6.8, while the Pacific Tsunami Warning System measured it at 7.0. Numerous aftershocks were recorded on Saturday, several reaching a magnitude of 6.07.

In response, Russian authorities issued tsunami alerts for three coastal districts. The Ministry for Emergency Services warned that even minimal waves could pose a risk to people on the shore. Estimated wave heights varied: up to 19 centimetres (7.5 inches) near the Kuril Islands, 15 centimetres in the Ust‑Kamchatsky district, 3 centimetres in the Petropavlovsk‑Kamchatsky urban district, and up to 19 centimetres in the Aleutsky district. Residents were advised to stay away from the coast.

The Main Directorate of the Ministry of Emergency Situations of Russia for Kamchatka Krai announced via Telegram that the tsunami threat to Kamchatka’s shores had been lifted, and Reuters noted that the alert had since been rescinded. No tsunami warning has been issued for Hawaii or any other part of the United States.

USGS and other agencies continue to monitor seismic activity and keep the public informed about events worldwide.

Revised on 8/3/2025 at 6:30 a.m. ET: This article now includes supplementary details.
