data.fused_content:
The Canyon Fire ignited at about 1:25 p.m. on Thursday, August 8, near the Lake Piru reservoir in Ventura County.  Initially covering roughly 30 acres, the blaze expanded rapidly and by Thursday evening had grown to 4,856 acres, challenging firefighters attempting to control it.

At 10:48 a.m. local time, Cal Fire issued a notice warning that the fire was advancing swiftly.  The department’s incident update declared the area “lawfully closed to public access” and urged residents to leave immediately.  Los Angeles County Supervisor Kathryn Barger tweeted, “Immediate threat to life. The area is lawfully closed to public access. — Keep aware — please don’t risk lives.”

Approximately 17,000 residents in Santa Clarita, Hasley Canyon, Hathaway Ranch and surrounding communities were instructed to evacuate.  The fire posed a danger to 60 kV power lines and threatened the nearby communities.

The National Interagency Coordination Center reported that, as of August 8, 42,670 fires had burned in the United States in 2025, exceeding the decade‑long average of 35,620 fires.  Earlier in the year, Los Angeles experienced widespread destruction from a string of unseasonal wildfires, and the Gifford Fire had become California’s second‑largest blaze.

Climate experts note that extreme weather events are becoming more frequent and intense.  A July report from the European Commission highlighted that extreme heat events, once occurring once every ten years, now happen almost three times as often and are about 1.2 °C (≈2.2 °F) hotter, a trend corroborated by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change.

Red Cross evacuation centers were opened to assist residents of Ventura and Los Angeles counties.  As of the latest update, containment of the Canyon Fire had reached 25 %.
