data.fused_content:
The Insurance Council of Australia (ICA) has warned that government intervention is essential to reduce the financial impact of extreme weather events that are becoming more frequent and severe because of climate change.  According to ICA spokesperson Kimberley Price, the financial toll from three disasters this year has climbed to nearly $2 billion.

In late October 2023, the township of Rochester, situated along the Campaspe River, suffered one of its most severe floods on record.  Floodwaters covered the floors of about a thousand homes, causing extensive damage.  Leigh Wilson, chairperson of the Rochester Community Recovery Committee, said the community has made a strong recovery, although a limited number of residents are still undergoing final processes.  He also noted a noticeable rise in insurance premiums and argued that greater accessibility to affordable insurance would benefit many communities.

The ICA’s latest report on insurance catastrophe resilience examines how climate change is poised to intensify weather events and the resulting economic repercussions.  It calls for a disaster‑preparedness fund to strengthen essential infrastructure, the elimination of insurance taxes to lower premiums, enhanced land‑use strategies, and targeted investment in resilience.  The report warns that failing to implement these measures will widen the divide between those who are adequately insured and those who are not.

Stakeholders echo the urgency of the ICA’s recommendations.  Greg Mullins, who established Emergency Leaders for Climate Action and formerly served as Commissioner for Fire and Rescue New South Wales, stresses that investing a single dollar in resilience can yield savings of up to $10 by reducing response and recovery costs.  Kylie Macfarlane, deputy chief executive of the ICA, points out that Australia’s distinct geographical features heighten its vulnerability to natural disasters.

The ICA urges state and federal authorities to act swiftly, establishing a disaster‑ready fund and adopting the report’s recommendations to protect communities and curb the escalating financial burden of climate‑related disasters.
