In 2025 the United States experienced 23 weather‑ and climate‑related disasters that each cost more than a billion dollars, the third‑highest total for a single year behind only 2023 and 2024.  The 23 events caused an estimated $115 billion in damage and 276 fatalities, far exceeding the historical average of nine billion‑dollar incidents per year and the typical annual cost of $67.6 billion.

The most expensive event was the January wildfires in Los Angeles, which inflicted $61.2 billion in damage.  According to Climate Central, the fires set a new record for wildfire disasters, almost twice the cost of the previous highest‑priced incident.  The March tornado outbreak was the costliest severe‑weather event of the year, with 180 tornadoes sweeping from Texas and Oklahoma through the Gulf Coast to North Carolina, causing $11 billion in damage and 43 deaths.  The West also suffered a severe drought driven by intense heat, which imposed about $3.1 billion in losses.

The frequency of billion‑dollar disasters has accelerated.  In 2025 the average interval between such events was just 10 days, compared with 16 days on average from 2016 to 2025 and 82 days during the 1980s.  Since 1980 the United States has recorded 426 weather and climate disasters that each cost a billion dollars or more, with cumulative damages exceeding $3.1 trillion.

“These events affect everyone, from higher insurance premiums to government disaster‑relief funding,” said Winkley.
