Peshawar, Pakistan – Torrential monsoon rains that began on June 26 have triggered flash floods and landslides in northern Pakistan, sweeping away several tourist‑laden vehicles and killing at least three people locally, with 15 missing. Nationwide, the rains have caused at least 225 deaths and more than 500 injuries, officials said.

A cloudburst on Monday caused floods and landslides that blocked a major highway. Army helicopters were deployed to evacuate stranded tourists and residents, while rescuers used heavy equipment to search for the missing. Over 200 local tourists were stranded, and several vehicles were entombed in landslide debris. Three bodies have been retrieved.

