The Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) has warned that the United Kingdom’s wheat crop has fallen by more than 7 million tonnes, a shortfall that equates to over four billion loaves of bread – enough to supply the country for an entire year.  Since 2020, the UK has lost a full year’s supply of bread because extreme weather has devastated harvests.

Climate‑driven droughts and floods have made the past decade the most difficult for British agriculture.  Two of the three poorest harvests on record have occurred this decade, with 2020 the worst and 2024 the third‑worst.  The ECIU predicts that 2025 will be another challenging year, as climate change is expected to bring extended dry spells alongside more intense rainfall that could trigger flooding.

The country’s wheat self‑sufficiency fell sharply, dropping from 96 % in 2023 to 79 % in 2024.  Millers have responded by importing unprecedented quantities of wheat, with imports last autumn rising to twice the five‑year average.  “This exemplifies the reality of farming amidst climate change,” said Tom Lancaster, an analyst for the ECIU.  “Unpredictable weather patterns are rendering our bread less British, compelling millers to rely on imports because of deficiencies in UK yields.  The scarcity of wheat is straining international supplies.”

Farmers on the ground echo the urgency.  Colin Chappell, a crop farmer in Lincolnshire, said, “Farming has never been more difficult.  While I am accustomed to handling the harshest weather, the recent years have turned unmanageable.  Extended periods of rain are succeeded by stretches of drought, appearing relentless, and I am now confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest.  I require additional assistance to adjust to these extreme conditions and fortify my business’s resilience.  However, with the green farming schemes in England closed, that support isn’t open to me.  Getting them open has to be a priority if farmers are to be able to continue to grow wheat and other key crops for the Great British public.”

The ECIU stresses that while farmers can enhance their efforts to adapt to harsh conditions, achieving net‑zero emissions is the only way to avert further weather‑related losses in the future.
