A wildfire that began on Tuesday in the Gold Rush town of Chinese Camp in Tuolumne County, California, has spread to an area of 10 square miles and prompted the evacuation of the town and nearby highways. CalFire, the state’s primary firefighting agency, says the blaze, which was ignited by a lightning strike, is part of a larger complex of fires that erupted across the state on Tuesday.

At least five residences—both standalone and mobile homes—have burned. A group of seven residents removed large tree branches and shoveled sand onto the flames for about 30 minutes before firefighters arrived, but the fire continued to spread. An RV on the property was damaged by the blaze.

Fire officials have requested additional resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft, but containment efforts have not yet succeeded as of Wednesday morning. No injuries or fatalities have been reported.

Chinese Camp was established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. The settlement, originally named Camp Washington, was later renamed to honor the influx of Chinese settlers who faced discrimination and hostility during the Gold Rush era.
